    summary: 'Students learn what a ratio is and use ratio language, equivalent ratios, diagrams, double number lines and tables to solve problems about recipes, speeds, prices and part-part-whole situations, ending with a Fermi problem.',
    bigIdeas: [
      'A ratio associates two quantities, written "a to b" or a:b, and can be represented with discrete diagrams.',
      'Equivalent ratios come from multiplying or dividing both quantities by the same number; recipes taste the same and colors match when ratios are equivalent.',
      'Double number lines and tables organize equivalent ratios and make it possible to find unknown values, including "per 1" values.',
      'Ratios describe constant speed and price and part-part-whole relationships (tape diagrams).',
    ],
